RECORDING 2
A late Swedish academic has identified a worrying trend.

133
00:20:37,320 --> 00:20:49,100
Not only do many people across advanced economies have no idea that the world is becoming a much better place, but they actually even think the opposite.

134
00:20:49,100 --> 00:20:58,760
This is no wonder, when the news focuses on reporting catastrophes, terrorist attacks, wars, and famines.

135
00:20:58,760 --> 00:21:08,820
Who wants to hear about the fact that every day some 200,000 people around the world are lifted above the two dollar a day poverty line?

136
00:21:08,820 --> 00:21:17,600
Or that more than 300,000 people get access to electricity and clean water for the first time every day?

137
00:21:17,600 --> 00:21:24,820
These stories of people in low income countries simply don't make for exciting news coverage.

138
00:21:24,820 --> 00:21:29,420
But as Rosling pointed out in his book Factfulness.

139
00:21:29,420 --> 00:21:33,840
It's important to put all the bad news in perspective.

140
00:21:33,840 --> 00:21:50,760
While it is true that globalization has put some downward pressure on middle class wages in advanced economies in recent decades, it has also helped lift hundreds of millions of people out of poverty.

141
00:21:50,760 --> 00:22:06,860
If we really care about global welfare, globalization is the only way forward to ensure that economic prosperity is shared among all countries and not only a select few advanced economies.

142
00:22:06,860 --> 00:22:19,690
While some people glorify the past, One of the big facts of economic history is that until quite recently, a significant part of the world's population has lived under quite miserable conditions.

143
00:22:19,690 --> 00:22:24,840
And this is true throughout most of human history.

144
00:22:24,840 --> 00:22:38,360
While there were many long lasting ups and downs, like the Great Depression or the recent Great Recession, the constancy of the long run growth rate is actually quite miraculous.

145
00:22:38,360 --> 00:22:49,620
Low income countries, including China and India, have been growing at a significantly faster pace in recent decades, and are quickly catching up to the West.

146
00:22:49,620 --> 00:22:57,240
A 10 percent growth rate over a prolonged period means that income levels double roughly every seven years.

147
00:22:57,240 --> 00:23:04,960
It is obviously good news if prosperity is more shared across the globe.

148
00:23:04,960 --> 00:23:16,980
While inequality within countries has gone up as a result of globalization, global inequality has been on a steady downward trend for several decades.

149
00:23:16,980 --> 00:23:23,840
This is mostly a result of the improvement of hundreds of millions of people's living standards.

150
00:23:23,840 --> 00:23:41,560
In fact, for the first time ever since the Industrial Revolution, about half of the global population can be considered middle class.

151
00:23:41,560 --> 00:24:00,880
Q19. What is the worrying trend the late Swedish academic has identified?

152
00:24:00,880 --> 00:24:18,840
Q20. What does the speaker say about globalization?

153
00:24:18,840 --> 00:24:38,020
Q21. What do we learn about half of the global population?

